  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Course Description</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The human skeletal system as an anatomical structure and biomechanical system. Lab experience in identification of osteological material and recognition of diseases associated with bone.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Course Learning Outcomes (CLO)</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Upon completion of the course the students will be able to identify all {{ total_bones }} bones in the human body.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Required Texts/Readings</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">White TD. {{ textbook_year }}. The Human Bone Manual. Elsevier. ISBN: {{ textbook_isbn }}</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Book is available at the University bookstore.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Course Requirements, Assignments, and Grades</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Success in this course is based on the expectation that students will spend, for each unit of credit, a minimum of forty-five hours over the length of the course (normally 3 hours per unit per week with 1 of the hours used for lecture) for instruction or preparation/studying or course related activities including but not limited to internships, labs, clinical practica. Other course structures will have equivalent workload expectations as described in the syllabus.</p>
</div>

